  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["J2", "U2"],
      "pins": ["J2.2", "J2.3", "U2.46", "U2.49"],
      "nets": ["NET0004", "NET0018"],
      "observed": "On the root sheet the SWD connector J2 pin 2 (SWCLK) carries net NET0004, which is labelled onto MCU pin PA13 (U2 pin 46), while J2 pin 3 (SWDIO) carries net NET0018, labelled onto MCU pin PA14 (U2 pin 49). The two SWD lines are therefore connected to the opposite MCU pins.",
      "expected": "On the STM32L476RGT6 the Serial Wire Debug pins are fixed by hardware: PA13 is SWDIO and PA14 is SWCLK (these are dedicated AF0 functions, not remappable). The SWDIO net should sit on PA13 and the SWCLK net on PA14, i.e. the two connections are swapped relative to sound design practice (the specification does not state the mapping, so this is judged by standard practice and the part's own AF table).",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Root sheet PCB.kicad_sch: J2 pin 2 'SWCLK' -> NET0004; J2 pin 3 'SWDIO' -> NET0018; MCU right column shows 'NET0004' on the PA13/46 row and 'NET0018' on the PA14/49 row."},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "J1 pins are VCC, GND, ~{RESET}, SWCLK, SWDIO, SWO — a standard ARM debug/programming interface (the spec lists the SWD signals but does not map them to MCU pins, leaving the fixed STM32 AF mapping to apply)."}
      ],
      "impact": "The programmer's clock is driven into the MCU's data pin and the data line into the clock pin, so SWD programming and debugging via the Tag-Connect header will fail (no device ID read, cannot flash).",
      "confidence": 0.5
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The NFC, display, touch, reset and power/decoupling sections all match the specification (correct pull-up values, I2C strapping BS0/BS1/BS2 = 0/1/0, D0/D1/D2 bus assignment, TSC 1k/22n, NRST pull-up, and the documented unresolved antenna tuning cap), but the SWD SWCLK and SWDIO nets appear swapped between the Tag-Connect header and the MCU's fixed PA13/PA14 pins, which would prevent programming/debug."
}

